encyclopedia :nacre do
  name "Nacre"
  summary "Nacre are a sapient people whose overlapping mineral plates take the kinetic grain of travel as fine grooves. Trained readers can recover bearings, burns, spin, and gravity passages from shed plates, which outer route holds preserve as voyage records."
  kind :lifeform
  origin_blurb "Overlapping mineral plates record travel as fine kinetic grooves and are shed in slow cycles."
  subkind :sapient_species
  status :complete
  topics :species, :navigation, :"outer-system"
  prevalence :uncommon
  available_globally
  registry true
  origin "A people of the outer routes; their route holds' plate libraries are the oldest records the long runs have, and the oldest plates in them are nacre"
  biology "Overlapping mineral plates across shoulders, back, and forearms, grown and shed in slow cycles; travel writes the journey's kinetic grain into the growing plate as fine grooves — bearings, burns, and gravity passages legible to a trained reader"
  lifespan "About 90–160 years; the plates of a long working life, kept in sequence, are a career readable end to end"
  resonance_relation "The plates take the kinetic band directly, recording a journey's acceleration, spin, and gravity passages as grooves legible under a trained fingertip"

  descriptive_identity(
    appearance: "Broad-framed, with overlapping mineral plates showing " \
                "pearl-gray at collar and forearm. A " \
                "traveled nacre's current plates carry visible groove-work, " \
                "while new growth begins smooth at the plate margins.",
    senses: "A nacre feels the kinetic band in the plates — course changes, " \
            "burns, and spin passages registered bodily — and can retrace a " \
            "journey from memory of how it wrote.",
    behavior: "Travel grooves form without effort, but reading fine details " \
              "requires practice. Outer route holds archive shed plates and " \
              "teach conventions for tracing another person's record with " \
              "permission."
  )

  cue "The young pilot asks after the passage and the old nacre unclasps a forearm plate, runs the pilot's thumb along one groove line — here the burn, here the bad spin — and the route crosses from shell to memory in a single tracing."
  cue "At the route hold's library the shed plates hang in career sequence, and the reader pulls one rack out for the insurance factor: the passage in dispute, written in the body of someone who flew it #{duration 60} ago."
  affordance "A shed plate preserves the kinetic course one person traveled. Pilots, courts, and insurers compare several plates with logs and instruments when reconstructing an outer passage."
  variation "Outer route holds maintain the largest plate libraries and reader schools. Shipboard and inner-system nacre produce the same grooves, whether or not they ever enter an archive."
  variation "Plate-reading is its own craft with ranked readers — most trace the working grooves, and the fine readers who can pull weather and load off an old plate serve the courts and the insurance tables as the atlas's living index."

  prose <<~PROSE
    Nacre grow overlapping pearl-gray mineral plates across the shoulders, back, and forearms. Each plate is shed and replaced on a slow cycle. While it grows, acceleration, spin, and passage through changing gravity mark its surface as fine groove structure. A nacre feels these forces through the plates as they occur and can usually retrace a familiar journey from bodily memory. Reading the precise course from a shed plate is a trained skill.

    Outer route holds preserve donated plates in career sequence. Trained readers trace bearings, burns, and gravity passages, then compare several travelers' plates to separate the shared course from maneuvers, load, injury, or a reader's interpretation. The oldest libraries form a continuous atlas of several long runs and are consulted by pilots, insurers, and courts. A route supported by many independent plates is described in the holds as *in the shell*.

    Grooves also record detours and private journeys. Route-hold libraries therefore require the wearer's permission before accepting a plate and restrict handling of current plates. Offering a guest one groove to trace can convey a bearing without exposing the rest. These are customs and rules of the route holds, not instincts carried by every nacre; people elsewhere keep, destroy, display, or ignore their shed plates according to household practice.
  PROSE
end
